{"id":278848292867275,"type":"song","name":"I Want to Be Your Joey Ramone","artist":"Sleater-Kinney","year":1996,"weight":10,"info":{"img":null},"artist_ref":{"id":57024380676905,"name":"Sleater-Kinney","info":{"img":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/artist/4b86517bdbd9628f85b3f4bf9316a19e/1000x1000-000000-80-0-0.jpg"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This song from their 1996 album \"The Doctor\" is a statement about the necessity for girls to have female role models. Sleater-Kinney, while not strictly Riot Grrrl, carried on the feminist message of the movement. \"It's called, I want to be your Joey Ramon.\"","year":1996,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Riot Grrrls - A Primer","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qss5kC2FUx0"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This track from the 1996 album *Call the Doctor* is highlighted as a piece that \"closes the loop\" on the broader discussion of punk rock, serving as an emblematic song. \"Sleeter Kinney and I Want to Be Your Joey Ramon from 1996.\"","year":1996,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The 90s Part 6: The Punk Revival","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0QtMI00T5jw"}]}],"book_tracks":[],"others":[{"id":265529254782760,"type":"album","name":"Call The Doctor","year":1995,"weight":31},{"id":122851661602864,"type":"album","name":"Dig Me Out","year":1997,"weight":22},{"id":204743300174707,"type":"album","name":"The Woods","year":2005,"weight":19},{"id":87674334878755,"type":"album","name":"One Beat","year":2002,"weight":13},{"id":86922012675409,"type":"album","name":"No Cities to Love","year":2015,"weight":8},{"id":75091285776987,"type":"song","name":"Bury Our Friends","year":2015,"weight":7},{"id":261244915949517,"type":"album","name":"The Hot Rock","year":1999,"weight":5},{"id":217216728754942,"type":"album","name":"The Doctor","year":1996,"weight":4},{"id":1405859